WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:02.833
Setup drafted everything
from evidence last clip,

00:00:02.833 --> 00:00:05.333
and it suggested a blueprint for you.

00:00:05.333 --> 00:00:09.067
Pick a motion now, and a working system
is drafted in one change: personas,

00:00:09.067 --> 00:00:11.067
scoring, suppression, lanes and plays.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.400
Over the next few minutes you'll
know what a blueprint installs,

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.033
how to preview and apply one, and
what stands between approved and live.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.067
Leo wires outbound one setting at a time.

00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:24.267
Weeks later the scoring, the suppression
list and the sequences disagree,

00:00:24.267 --> 00:00:26.000
and the change history is a guess.

00:00:26.033 --> 00:00:27.867
A motion is the way you go to market.

00:00:27.867 --> 00:00:29.800
A blueprint is a shipped template that

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.767
seeds a whole motion in one ChangeSet,

00:00:31.767 --> 00:00:33.567
a bundle of changes reviewed together.

00:00:33.567 --> 00:00:36.767
Blueprints are general templates,
never forked per customer.

00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:39.667
Hold on to this: a blueprint is
a whole motion in one change,

00:00:39.667 --> 00:00:41.233
and approved is not live.

00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:43.167
First, browse. In the console: Blueprints.

00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:47.333
The gallery has industry packages and
starter blueprints, a motion filter,

00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:51.100
and search. Signed and verified badges
tell you where each one comes from.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.200
Or ask your agent: "List the blueprints".

00:00:53.233 --> 00:00:57.900
The motions include: full go to market,
outbound, SEO, sales led B2B SaaS,

00:00:57.900 --> 00:00:58.367
product-led.

00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:02.933
Then developer tools, direct to consumer,
agency services and marketplaces.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.500
If you're not sure which fits, setup
ranks blueprints from your motions,

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:10.267
ARR band and team size. It shows every
fact it used and every fact against.

00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:13.800
Or ask your agent: "Which blueprint
fits us and what does it need".

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.167
It answers with fit,
requirements and gaps.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.500
Second, read before you apply. Open
any blueprint and click View and apply.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:24.933
What it sets up lists everything it
will draft: personas, segments, scoring,

00:01:24.933 --> 00:01:28.533
suppression rules, bindings, lanes,
plays, metrics and sequences.

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.500
Engines it drives names
the engines behind it.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.667
What you need connected marks
each connection Required, Ready,

00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:38.467
Optional or Missing. Those requirements
flow into your readiness check,

00:01:38.467 --> 00:01:40.667
so gtm doctor tells you the same thing.

00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:43.500
Full go to market requires
a model, an email sender,

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.433
looot and Search Console,
while outbound needs a model,

00:01:46.433 --> 00:01:48.067
an email sender and looot.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.067
Search blueprints need Search Console.

00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:51.900
Without it the search plays are held back,

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.900
and you apply again once it's connected.

00:01:53.967 --> 00:01:56.933
Third, plan, then apply. Pro
move: run the plan first.

00:01:56.933 --> 00:01:59.633
In the terminal: gtm plan with
the from flag and a blueprint.

00:01:59.633 --> 00:02:03.067
Or ask your agent: "Preview what the
outbound blueprint would change".

00:02:03.067 --> 00:02:06.833
You and your approver read the same
list before anything is proposed.

00:02:06.833 --> 00:02:09.233
If a requirement is missing, fix it first.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:12.600
Now apply. In the console:
Blueprints, then Apply this blueprint.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:16.200
Choose an environment. It defaults to
dev, the lowest one below production,

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.533
because production
refuses direct publishes.

00:02:18.533 --> 00:02:21.300
Outreach blueprints ask for
your sender's postal address.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:24.000
Click Apply, or run gtm
apply with environment dev.

00:02:24.033 --> 00:02:25.500
Applying validates the blueprint,

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:28.267
drafts one change for everything
it sets up, and proposes it.

00:02:28.267 --> 00:02:30.733
Or ask your agent: "Apply the
outbound blueprint to dev".

00:02:30.733 --> 00:02:33.133
You get an approval number,
and the agent stops,

00:02:33.133 --> 00:02:34.500
because an agent never approves.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.733
Apply again while that proposal waits,
and you get the same proposal back,

00:02:37.733 --> 00:02:38.633
not a duplicate.

00:02:38.667 --> 00:02:40.633
Fourth, approve, sign, promote.

00:02:40.633 --> 00:02:43.233
Sophia opens the approval card:
the frozen payload as a diff,

00:02:43.233 --> 00:02:45.700
the impact preview, and who proposed it.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:47.467
She can't approve her own proposals,

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:49.433
and neither can the
person an agent acts for.

00:02:49.433 --> 00:02:51.967
When she approves, the
change executes in dev.

00:02:52.033 --> 00:02:55.400
Watch this one. Approved is not
live. The definitions land in dev,

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:59.067
then someone other than the proposer
signs them, and then you promote.

00:02:59.067 --> 00:03:02.267
Environments have their own clip:
dev, staging and production.

00:03:02.333 --> 00:03:05.233
Once signed and promoted,
engines read the signed version,

00:03:05.233 --> 00:03:06.833
and until then they use their defaults.

00:03:06.833 --> 00:03:10.567
Any external effect still needs a
person's approval, and respects Dry Mode.

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:11.633
Fifth, customize.

00:03:11.633 --> 00:03:14.933
Values your organization owns come
from your approved setup cards.

00:03:14.933 --> 00:03:17.100
ICP weights, stages, customer types,

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:20.433
suppression lists and the daily cap
are adapted to what you approved,

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:22.100
not left as template defaults.

00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:25.233
In the console: use the
Settings pages such as Journeys,

00:03:25.233 --> 00:03:27.900
Money and the Proof library,
and the Environments page,

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:30.333
where each change stays
a draft until signed.

00:03:30.367 --> 00:03:31.333
Or ask your agent:

00:03:31.333 --> 00:03:33.000
"Adapt the outbound blueprint to our

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:34.867
enterprise segment and show me the plan".

00:03:34.867 --> 00:03:37.867
Claude drafts the change, you see
the diff and the impact preview,

00:03:37.867 --> 00:03:39.433
and a different person approves.

00:03:39.467 --> 00:03:43.100
In the terminal: gtm plan, gtm
apply with environment dev,

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:46.800
and gtm blueprint upgrade,
which pulls upstream updates.

00:03:46.867 --> 00:03:49.800
The objection: someone else's
template running my go to market?

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:50.833
Not until you say so.

00:03:50.833 --> 00:03:54.000
It lands as drafts in dev,
adapted to your approved values,

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:57.033
and engines ignore drafts. They
read only the signed version.

00:03:57.067 --> 00:04:00.933
Keep these: Browse, read what it
installs and needs, plan then apply,

00:04:00.933 --> 00:04:02.833
then approve, sign and promote.

00:04:02.833 --> 00:04:07.233
A blueprint is a whole motion in one
change, and approved is not live.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:10.300
You picked a motion, and now you
have a reviewed, proposed system.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:12.367
Next is connections and integrations,

00:04:12.367 --> 00:04:14.667
where you plug in everything
the blueprint requires.
